WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.488 --> 00:00:08.993
Priya Raman: Hi Mark, how are you doing?

2
00:00:09.583 --> 00:00:12.712
Mark Delgado: Hey Priya. Good, busy but good. How about you?

3
00:00:14.077 --> 00:00:22.491
Priya Raman: Pretty good, thanks. Let me pull up the account while we talk. How have call volumes been looking this month?

4
00:00:23.254 --> 00:00:32.474
Mark Delgado: Up a bit. We're running around 19,400 calls a month across the 38 clinics now, which is maybe six percent over August.

5
00:00:32.474 --> 00:00:34.569
Mark Delgado: Scheduling's been the driver, mostly.

6
00:00:34.892 --> 00:00:44.773
Priya Raman: That tracks with what I saw in the dashboard. Is central scheduling still the same group on Genesys, or did anything shift there?

7
00:00:45.883 --> 00:00:57.576
Mark Delgado: Same group. We did move two of the float agents onto a permanent schedule, so coverage is a little steadier during the 11 to 1 lunch crunch.

8
00:00:57.984 --> 00:01:06.505
Priya Raman: Good, that was the window we talked about last time. Anything else operational I should know about before we get into the agenda?

9
00:01:07.905 --> 00:01:16.854
Mark Delgado: One thing. We're adding a clinic in Murfreesboro in January, so we'll need to get tracking numbers set up for them at some point.

10
00:01:16.854 --> 00:01:17.973
Mark Delgado: Probably early December.

11
00:01:19.347 --> 00:01:28.317
Priya Raman: Okay, noted. I'll put a placeholder on my calendar for early December and we can walk through the number provisioning then.

12
00:01:28.317 --> 00:01:30.880
Priya Raman: Anything on your side that's blocked?

13
00:01:31.338 --> 00:01:35.603
Mark Delgado: Nothing blocked, no. Honestly the day to day is fine.

14
00:01:36.172 --> 00:01:46.651
Priya Raman: Great. I had two things from our last conversation, and one of them was about the QM scorecards. You mentioned you wanted to show me something.

15
00:01:47.604 --> 00:01:56.176
Mark Delgado: Yeah, so this is the main thing I wanted to talk through. Our human QA team has a rubric they've used for years.

16
00:01:56.176 --> 00:02:04.003
Mark Delgado: It has very specific language for the exact disclosures agents have to read, and it scores differently depending on call type.

17
00:02:04.256 --> 00:02:09.230
Priya Raman: Okay. Can you say a little more about the call type part?

18
00:02:09.548 --> 00:02:17.841
Mark Delgado: Sure. A new patient scheduling call is scored differently than a refill request or a billing question. Ten points on one, five on another.

19
00:02:17.841 --> 00:02:23.716
Mark Delgado: And within each type certain criteria are weighted heavier. Right now we can't reproduce any of that.

20
00:02:24.665 --> 00:02:32.280
Priya Raman: So what you're scoring against today, is it close to the human rubric, or is it a different set of questions entirely?

21
00:02:33.138 --> 00:02:45.661
Mark Delgado: Different. It's directionally useful but the criteria don't line up, so when my QA lead Denise pulls a report the numbers don't match what her team is seeing in their own reviews.

22
00:02:45.661 --> 00:02:54.271
Mark Delgado: We'd want to define our own criteria, our own weightings, and have it vary by call type. Essentially mirror their existing rubric.

23
00:02:54.841 --> 00:03:00.424
Priya Raman: Got it. That makes sense. How many criteria do they have, roughly, and how many call types?

24
00:03:01.595 --> 00:03:07.573
Mark Delgado: Four call types, and between eight and twelve criteria each. There's some overlap but not a lot.

25
00:03:07.869 --> 00:03:16.369
Priya Raman: Okay. Let me write this up as a request and send it over with the details so the product team has the rubric shape.

26
00:03:16.369 --> 00:03:18.849
Priya Raman: Can you send me the actual rubric?

27
00:03:19.488 --> 00:03:22.964
Mark Delgado: Yeah, I'll get that to you tomorrow. It's a spreadsheet.

28
00:03:24.036 --> 00:03:30.875
Priya Raman: Perfect. That'll help a lot. How urgent is it? Asking so I can flag it correctly.

29
00:03:32.362 --> 00:03:46.912
Mark Delgado: Medium, I'd say. It's not breaking anything. It's just that we're doing double the work, running their rubric and then looking at ours, and it'd be nice to have one number leadership trusts whole.

30
00:03:47.841 --> 00:03:57.110
Priya Raman: Understood. I'll get it in this week. Last thing on my list, and it's quick. How's the Salesforce Health Cloud sync been behaving?

31
00:03:57.883 --> 00:03:59.605
Mark Delgado: Fine. No complaints there.

32
00:04:00.789 --> 00:04:02.469
Priya Raman: Good. And Google Ads?

33
00:04:03.933 --> 00:04:04.733
Mark Delgado: Also fine.

34
00:04:05.038 --> 00:04:09.774
Priya Raman: Okay, that's the whole agenda. Anything you want to add before we wrap?

35
00:04:11.265 --> 00:04:14.401
Mark Delgado: No, I think that's it. Thanks for the time.

36
00:04:14.663 --> 00:04:19.834
Priya Raman: Of course. I'll send the write-up and the December calendar hold. Talk soon, Mark.

37
00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:23.059
Mark Delgado: Sounds good. Bye, Priya.
